{"type": "thinking", "text": "Reopened Add: Type Basic, Deck Spanish — deck reverted to Spanish too. So both pickers reverted after back (no save). So the deck picker isn't persisted across cancel either. Maybe that's expected: opening Add note resets to \"last added note\" defaults. So the \"stored straight away\" change might refer to something else.\n\nHmm. In AnkiDroid, Add Note's deck defaults to the previously selected deck only if you saved a note? Let me test: add a note with French deck + Cloze type, then reopen: will it remember French/Cloze? If yes, persistence works on save. Then the \"stored straight away\" claim means the switch is stored when... hmm.\n\nMaybe \"Add note pickers\" refers to the note-type picker in the *note editor when editing an existing note*? No, that's \"note editor\".\n\nLet me think about the change semantics: In AnkiDroid 2.16+, the Add Note screen has a \"note type\" spinner and deck spinner. When you change the note type, the fields change. The selected model is stored in the collection config `currentNoteType`... I recall code: `addNoteFragment` has `onNoteTypeSelected` calls `addNoteViewModel.setNoteType(...)` and stores `deckId`? There's a preference `lastDeckId`. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the stored-straight-away behavior manifests in the *note type spinner default when adding another note after adding one*. Let me test: add a Cloze note in French, then tap Add again (after saving) → check Type/Deck. That's the normal \"remember last used\" behavior which existed before. The change says switch is stored straight away — maybe when switching model, the note type's field content is saved into the collection so reopening Add note (even after kill) shows it. Our back-out test shows not. But maybe back-out intentionally resets to last saved defaults (i.e., last note type used when a note was added). That could be considered correct design: only \"committed\" note types become defaults.\n\nHmm, but the change explicitly says \"a switch of note model is now stored to the collection straight away\" — that implies on switching, not on saving. Our observation contradicts it. Candidate bug.\n\nTo strengthen: maybe test with app restart (force stop?) — if switching then rotating or going to another screen and back within the same Add flow? Actually \"stored to the collection straight away\" could mean: within the same Add session, if you switch model, then press back and reopen Add, the model should be remembered (because stored). It isn't.\n\nAlternatively, maybe switching to a model that has a different field set (Cloze) while there is unsaved content triggers a \"discard?\" dialog; not our case.\n\nLet me try a variation: open Add, switch to Cloze, then instead of Back, save a note. Then reopen Add → type should be Cloze (remembered last used). Then switch to Basic within that new Add session, then switch back to Cloze? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me test whether changing note type then *rotating* keeps it (state) — a basic state test.\n\nBut more important: is the failure to persist after cancel a real bug? Let me check the actual implementation intent by searching memory: AnkiDroid's Add note screen: `AddNoteFragment` obtains `noteTypeId` default from `addNoteViewModel.getNoteTypeId()` which is `col.getNotetypeId()`? There is `Collection.getCurrentNoteType`? In AnkiDroid, there is `col.get_config(\"lastNoteType\", ...)`? Hmm. In recent AnkiDroid, when adding a note, the selected note type is persisted via `plainTextEdit`? I vaguely recall preference keys: \"lastDeckId\", \"lastNoteType\"? Hmm.\n\nGiven the change list is generated from actual code changes in the build, \"Add note pickers: a switch of note model is now stored to the collection straight away.\" So the bug seeded is likely that persistence is missing/broken. Our test is decent evidence but a developer may reject if expected behavior is different.\n\nLet me examine more carefully by testing the picker switch persistence in another way: In Add screen, switching model may be supposed to update the default note type for future additions *immediately*, so even opening a new note from a different route (e.g., FAB from deck list) should show the new model. Let's test route: from deck list FAB \"Add\" (after switching model?). Hmm.\n\nSequence: switch model to Cloze, then leave Add via back to browser, then go to deck list and tap FAB Add → check type. If it shows Basic again, persisted nowhere.\n\nAlternatively maybe rotating while in Add after switching shows it retained; then back/reopen resets. Rotation tests state.\n\nI think the bug is plausible enough. But before reporting, reproduce exactly once more (as instructed) and capture evidence: switch to Cloze, exit, reopen → \"Basic\" shown. I already did twice (Cloze→Basic; Basic-and-reversed→Basic). Good.\n\nHmm wait: In the first instance, I switched to \"Basic (and reversed card)\" while front field empty, then pressed back once. The screen exited promptly to browser (observed). Then Add reopened → Basic. Second: switched to Cloze, pressed back once (keyboard closed only), pressed back again → browser; reopened Add → Basic. So both show reversion.\n\nBut here's a catch: In the first case I pressed back once, which exited; but maybe the note editor had focus in Front field and keyboard hidden? The screen exited. Fine.\n\nNow, what would a correct implementation produce? If model switch were stored, reopening would show Cloze. It doesn't. So bug: \"Add note note-model switch is not persisted; next Add note reverts to Basic.\"\n\nHmm, but maybe the switch IS stored to collection but the Add screen initializes from a different preference that gets set only after save. Then the developer's change \"stored straight away\" wouldn't show anyway. I'd report as not_persisted.\n\nLet me also check deck: the deck picker selection didn't persist on cancel either. But the change is only about note model. Maybe AnkiDroid intentionally resets deck to last saved. So the model resetting could also be by design (\"last saved model\"). Ugh.\n\nActually let's reconsider: The phrase \"Add note pickers: a switch of note model is now stored to the collection straight away.\" It could mean that when the user changes the note type, this \"default note type\" is stored; when the user changes the deck, the default deck is stored. The seeded bug might be that it's stored to the *wrong* place or not at all. Testing via reopen Add is the natural test.\n\nLet me check if maybe switching model and *saving* a note persists; then the difference would be \"stored on save, not straight away\". Let's test to understand behavior. Add a Cloze note (need cloze syntax to save successfully). I'll create content \"{{c1::test}}\". But that adds a note to collection (test data modification). We can delete it after. That's allowed for testing.\n\nActually better: use \"Basic (and reversed card)\" with Front \"tmpfront\"/Back \"tmpback\", save in French. Then it creates 2 cards; delete note afterward. But before adding, switched model in a fresh Add screen: change to Basic (and reversed card), fill fields, save. Then reopen Add and check if Type remembered as \"Basic (and reversed card)\". That tests save-persistence.\n\nHmm, but if the switch is stored \"straight away\", then even without save it should be remembered. Our evidence already shows not. I'll report after one more clean reproduction.\n\nWait — maybe there's another subtlety: after switching model, the note type is stored, BUT opening Add a second time loads \"Basic\" because the *previous* Add screen was cancelled and it reverts to the \"last saved note's type\". That's essentially the same bug from user perspective: switching model isn't remembered.\n\nLet me do the reproduction cleanly: currently Add screen fresh with Basic/Spanish. Switch to Cloze, observe spinner Cloze, press back twice to exit, tap Add again, observe spinner Basic. Then file report. Actually I'm already at the state where Add reopened with Basic/Spanish. Let me do it."}
