{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed: after tapping \"deck\" radio, the header still says \"French\", the search is \"deck:current\", but the data shows \"Studied 4 cards\" and Future Due total (Spanish). So the header French disagrees with the data (Spanish). This is a reproducible inconsistency.\n\nIs this a bug worth reporting? The deck title (French) is the stats screen's selected deck; after switching to \"deck\" mode it should show French stats. Instead it shows Spanish. And the radio indicates deck mode. This is a state/navigation inconsistency. Hmm.\n\nBut wait, maybe \"deck:current\" resolves to the deck selected in the deck list, which is Spanish (we opened the Card browser for Spanish and stats from there; the \"current deck\" is Spanish). The stats screen title is French because we chose French from the picker. In AnkiDroid, the statistics \"deck:current\" search term is explicitly \"the current deck\" (the one selected in the deck list). The title bar deck picker chooses the deck for the stats *when you use the deck selector*, setting search deck:\"French\". When you then choose \"deck\" radio, it changes the search to deck:current which is Spanish. The header not updating might be expected because the header shows the stats-deck (French) while the search term is deck:current. Hmm, that's confusing but could be upstream behavior.\n\nActually, in AnkiDroid, I recall the stats screen has a deck name in the toolbar and a dropdown; the \"deck\"/\"collection\" radio are labeled as search presets. When the search text is deck:current, the title may still show the deck name from the intent. Hmm.\n\nRisky. Let me set this aside and check the *charts* for issues with different data sets. Actually, let me revisit the hint: \"Study statistics screen: the charts are now built by the backend statistics renderer instead of native views.\" This tells us the rendering path changed; a planted bug could be that the charts show **wrong data or wrong labels** compared to the summary text. We've checked the summary text and chart axis; all consistent.\n\nWait, maybe the bug is that the charts are **not clickable/responsive** or **don't show the percentage line**... Hard.\n\nLet me look at the \"Hours\" chart more carefully: title \"Review success rate for each hour of the day\", y-axis right is %. The bar height 4 uses left axis counts. There should be a success-rate line at 100% at hour 11? If the line is missing, that's the bug. Let me zoom into the hourly chart image. I can't zoom but can crop by taking screenshots and viewing. The screenshot showed only a blue bar; no line. But with a single data point, a line would be a dot/point. Maybe invisible.\n\nAlternatively, \"Review Intervals\": the chart is an area (cumulative) and the summary \"Median interval: 1 day\" — but what about the 95% cutoff marker? At \"95%\" selected, the chart shows blue area until x=2 (95th percentile = 2 days? for 2 cards with 1-day interval, 95% percentile should be 1 day, not 2!). Hmm! Wait: with two review cards both with interval 1 day, the distribution: 100% of cards have interval 1 day. The 95% percentile of intervals should be 1 day (the maximum is 1). The chart, however, shows the blue (0-95%) region extending from x=1 to x=2, and the gray (95-100%) from x=2 to x=3. That implies the 95th percentile is at 2 days and the max at 3 days — which doesn't match our data (intervals are 1 day). Hmm, but does the \"Review Intervals\" x-axis represent days? The x-axis labels 1,1.2,...,3. With intervals of 1 day, the distribution should be a spike at 1. The chart's blue area from 1 to 2 looks wrong; also the \"95%\" line at x=2.\n\nWait, the x-axis might be \"interval in days\" but the chart is plotted as a *cumulative distribution* where the x-axis is the interval and the y-axis is cumulative %? But the y-axis shown 0..2 (left) and 0..2 (right)! Both axes 0..2. Hmm, for a cumulative distribution, y-axis should be percentage (0-100%). Here both left and right y axes are 0..2 (counts). That's odd.\n\nActually, the Review Intervals chart's left/right axes in our screenshots: left 0,0.5,1,1.5,2 and right 0,0.5,1,1.5,2. Both same. The blue area occupies x from 1 to 2 and y 0..2 — like a cumulative count curve rising from 0 at x=1 to 2 at x=2? Hmm, the fill under the curve: at x=1, curve y=0; at x=2, curve y≈2; then flat to 3. So the curve rises linearly from (1,0) to (2,2). That suggests the intervals are 1..2 days (i.e., the 2 cards have intervals 1 and 2 days?). Wait, maybe one card has interval 1 day and another 2 days? Let's check: agua and comer were set due tomorrow; their intervals after \"set due date\" maybe 1 day and ... hmm, they were learning cards at 10-minute steps; setting due date converted to review? Actually the SetDueDate on a learning card sets due date but keeps type learning? Hmm, maybe agua's interval became 1 day, comer's 2 days? Their \"Due\" both 2026-11-15 though. Interval might be 1 and 2 days due to ease? Hmm.\n\nWait, earlier Card Ease chart showed 2 cards at 250%. Review Intervals median 1 day. The distribution could be one card at 1 day and one at 2 days? Median of [1,2] = 1.5, displayed \"1 day\" (integer truncation?) Hmm. If intervals are 1 and 2, median = 1.5 → displayed 1? The chart 95% cutoff at 2 would be right (max 2). And the curve from 1 to 2 would be: at x=1, cumulative 1 card? Hmm.\n\nThe area chart: filled blue from x=1 to x=2 with top boundary rising from y=0 to y=2. If it's a cumulative distribution of review counts: at x=1 there's 1 card (cumulative 1?) but curve starts 0 at x=1?? Confusing.\n\nLet me check card info for agua and comer to get their intervals. Earlier mesa was learning (10 minutes). agua/comer were set due tomorrow. Let me check their card info: maybe intervals 1 and 2 days.\n\nActually, when you use \"Set due date\" on a learning card, Anki converts it to a review card with due date and sets interval based on... \"Set due date\" typically sets the due date but also changes interval to match days until due (if \"Set interval to same value\" not checked, interval stays?). Hmm, the dialog had a checkbox \"Set interval to same value\" which we did NOT check. So interval stays 0? For a learning card, interval maybe 0. Then the cards might be \"review\" type with interval 1 day? The card counts showed Young 2, so they're review cards with interval < 21 days. Their interval could be 1 day each (due tomorrow = 1 day). Hmm.\n\nLet me check card info for agua to see interval. Go to browser → long press agua → card info. But first, let me finish stats investigation? I can navigate.\n\nActually, maybe the \"Review Intervals\" chart is fine; I shouldn't over-interpret without knowing intervals. Let me get the intervals.\n\nLet me back to browser and check card info for agua and comer."}
